# Auth / guard changes — challenge before code

## Canonical model (Agent Studio)

- **Merged:** mesh allow-list (#274) gates **which principals** may call east-west.
- **App guard:** JWT with `email` → enforce user scope only; **no email / no token → `next()`**.
- **Deferred:** mesh DENY for JWT on sensitive paths → separate hardening PR (#320), not mixed into guard PRs.
- **Active spec:** `simplified-guard-mesh-first.md`. Do not reintroduce `internalAllowed`, `*Dual` policy keys, `/internal` URL twins, or XFCC/`X-Service-Caller` parsing unless the user explicitly requests them.

## Two phases (default)

**Phase 1 — user has not said `go`:** Read the cited design doc only. Answer: minimum implementation, what to delete from the old approach, risks of deferred work, file list + rough size. **No edits, no commits.**

**Phase 2 — user said `go`:** Implement **only** the approved slice. Respect file allow-list, test list, and non-goals from the prompt. Stop when tests pass; do not "finish" unrelated doc sections.

## Minimize diff

- Prefer deleting redundant machinery over adding policy keys or route twins.
- If two policy names are byte-identical, use one name.
- If > ~300 lines changed, stop and explain before continuing.
- One PR = one architectural decision (app guard vs mesh DENY vs SA-token removal).

## When user asks "implement &lt;doc&gt;"

Stop and ask (or answer in prose) whether Phase 1 is done. If the doc conflicts with mesh-first, say which wins — do not implement the heavier model by default.

Prompt template: `docs/design/learning/cursor-two-phase-implementation.md`
